  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Youth-led Green Transition through Urban Agri-business growth</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Zimbabwe&#x2019;s rapidly urbanising cities face worsening climate impacts, especially severe water shortages. There are also limited opportunities for youth employment. These pressures highlight the need for stronger support systems that connect youth to climate-resilient, sustainable urban food and business ecosystems. Building on the successes and lessons of Phase I, the project deepens support for youth-led agribusinesses now ready to scale and export. The project aims to strengthen youth-led agribusinesses to become export-ready, adopt resource-efficient green production, and benefit from improved policies and business environments. It will support 60 established youth agribusinesses with capacity building, mentoring, and small investments to enhance export readiness; train 40 additional youth businesses on water- and energy-efficient production using practical demonstration sites; and coordinate advocacy with sector partners and local authorities to promote sustainable consumption and improve the ease of doing business. As a result, youth-led enterprises will grow their markets, reduce resource use, and contribute more strongly to a green, inclusive urban economy.</narrative>
  </description>
